# Optional udev rule: pin an Arduino-class board to a stable device path
# (/dev/pinctl-board0) instead of relying on kernel enumeration order
# (/dev/ttyACM0, /dev/ttyACM1, ...), which can change across reboots or
# replugs — especially with more than one board attached. See
# docs/14-deployment.md#udev-and-serial-access for the full explanation.
#
# Not installed by deploy/install.sh — this is a per-board, per-operator
# rule (it embeds one board's USB serial number), so it's opt-in.
#
# Setup:
#
#   1. Find the board's USB serial number:
#        udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyACM0 | grep -m1 '{serial}'
#        # ATTRS{serial}=="85033313938351E0A162"
#
#   2. Copy this file to /etc/udev/rules.d/99-pinctl.rules, uncomment the
#      line below, and replace idVendor/serial with your board's values.
#      (idVendor=="2341" is Arduino's USB vendor ID; check yours via the
#      udevadm command above if you're using a clone or another vendor.)
#
#   3. Reload rules:
#        sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
#   4. Reference /dev/pinctl-board0 in /etc/pinctl/config.yaml's
#      serial.allowlist instead of /dev/ttyACM0.
#
# For a second board, duplicate the line with a different serial and a
# different SYMLINK (e.g. pinctl-board1).

# SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2341", ATTRS{serial}=="85033313938351E0A162", SYMLINK+="pinctl-board0"
